      "text": "1. We start by understanding the given properties of the function \\( f \\):\n   \\[\n   f(x,x) = x, \\quad f(x,y) = f(y,x), \\quad (x + y) f(x,y) = y f(x, x + y).\n   \\]\n2. We want to find \\( f(14, \\, 52) \\).\n\n3. Apply the second property to set \\( x = 14 \\) and \\( y = 52 \\):\n   \\[\n   f(14, 52) = f(52, 14).\n   \\]\n\n4. Use the third property with \\( x = 14 \\) and \\( y = 54 \\):\n   \\[\n   (14 + 54) f(14, 54) = 54 f(14, 68).\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   68 f(14, 54) = 54 f(14, 68).\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( f(14, 54) \\) and \\( f(14, 68) \\):\n   \\[\n   f(14, 54) = \\frac{54}{68} f(14, 68).\n   \\]\n5. Try again, setting \\( x = 14 \\) and \\( y = 72 \\) to find relationships:\n   \\[\n   (14 + 72) f(14, 72) = 72 f(14, 86).\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   86 f(14, 72) = 72 f(14, 86).\n   \\]\n\n6. Repeat with \\( x = 14 \\) and \\( y = 86 \\):\n   \\[\n   (14 + 86) f(14, 86) = 86 f(14, 100).\n   \\]\n   Seizing this opportunity, set \\( x = 14 \\) and \\( y = 100 \\):\n   \\[\n   (14 + 100) f(14, 100) = 100 f(14, 114).\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   114 f(14, 100) = 100 f(14, 114).\n   \\]\n7. Lastly, use \\( x = 14 \\) and \\( y = 114 \\):\n   \\[\n   (14 + 114) f(14, 114) = 114 f(14, 128).\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   128 f(14, 114) = 114 f(14, 128).\n   \\]\n\n8. Simplification leads to the observation that from these steps, we see that:\n   \\[\n   f(y, x) = \\frac{y}{x} f(x, y).\n   \\]\n   Thus, \n   \\[\n   f(14, 52) = f(14, 114) = 14.\n   \\] \n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]",
